      "easy javascript soln. TheCoderNextDoor 25 4439 Oct 03, 2022 JavaScript var   getConcatenation   =   function ( nums )   { \n      const  result  =   [ ] ; \n      for ( let  i =   0 ;   i <  nums . length ;  i ++ )   { \n         result [ i ]   =  nums [ i ] ; \n         result  [ i  +  nums . length ]   =  nums [ i ] \n      } \n      return  result ; \n } ; Comments (7) Shashank357 Mar 25, 2024 Very elegant solution. thanks. did not think this was possible so I used 2 pointer for this question. 0 1 zobee Aug 17, 2023 Alternatively, you can assign like this: \n ans[i + n] = ans[i] = nums[i]; 0 Collapse745 May 12, 2023 var getConcatenation = function(nums) { \nreturn nums.concat(nums); \n}; 0 JoelMashana Feb 07, 2023 Nice work 0 hasanmd91 Nov 25, 2022 very smart 0 kambangsinclaire Oct 30, 2022 smart 0 adz__1 Oct 14, 2022 appreciate itt 0",
      "🚀🚀 Beats 100% | 4 Lines of Code | 0ms 🔥🔥 Sanket_Deshmukh_24 884 5289 Jan 26, 2024 C++ Java Python C 3+ Complexity \n \n Time complexity: O(N) \n \n\n \n Space complexity: O(1) \n \n\n Code \n class   Solution   { \n public : \n     vector < int >   getConcatenation ( vector < int > &  nums )   { \n          int  n  =  nums . size ( ) ; \n \n          for ( int  i  =   0 ;  i  <  n ;  i ++ ) \n          { \n             nums . push_back ( nums [ i ] ) ; \n          } \n \n          return  nums ; \n      } \n } ; \n <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/5fd6e8b3-c7ff-4e68-a3ea-7029c3d19678_1706264939.3442893.png\" alt=\"lc1.png\"> Comments (3) Kewl_Rishab Jan 28, 2024 Where is java here? and you have tagged java .Stop fooling people. 37 ravindersinghcodess Mar 30, 2024 is it a good practice to change the given input ? 1 ScharmhaarKonig Feb 03, 2024 tbh I only saw the C++ answer, but it helped as a JS dev thx tho 0",
      "✅Java, PHP, Python, C++, JavaScript || Simple Solution || ✅Runtime 90% || ayeshakalsoom06 191 1743 Oct 04, 2023 C++ Java Python JavaScript 1+ Intuition \n The intuition behind this solution is straightforward. We want to concatenate the given array with itself to create a new array that has the same elements repeated twice. \n Approach \n \n \n We start by initializing an empty list called ans, which we'll use to store the concatenated array. \n \n \n We iterate through the elements of the input nums array using a for loop. For each element num in nums, we append it to the ans list. \n \n \n After the loop, we concatenate the nums array again to the ans list using the += operator. This effectively adds all the elements of nums to the end of ans. \n \n \n Finally, we return the ans list, which now contains the concatenated array. \n \n \n Complexity \n \n \n Time complexity: \nO(n) - We iterate through the input nums array once, where n is the length of nums. \n \n \n Space complexity: \nWe use additional space to store the ans list, which has the same length as nums. \n \n \n Code \n class   Solution : \n      def   getConcatenation ( self ,  nums ) : \n          # Initialize an empty list to store the concatenated array \n         ans  =   [ ] \n         \n          # Iterate through the elements of the input 'nums' array \n          for  num  in  nums : \n              # Append the current number to the 'ans' list \n             ans . append ( num ) \n         \n          # Concatenate 'nums' again to 'ans' to form the final result \n         ans  +=  nums\n         \n          return  ans\n } ; \n <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/e1a71172-683c-4457-b7f0-bbd3f106121e_1696442693.9413323.jpeg\" alt=\"478xve.jpg\"> Comments (2) ushnanayab483 Oct 05, 2023 Thank you. 3 james254mumo Mar 19, 2024 @ayeshakalsoom06 am curious what was your submission running time \n because when you do ans.append() inside a loop it is so inefficent and keeps making another copy of the list each time 0",
      "[JS/TS] Beats > 90% | Super Simple Fixed Array length approach spicedNoodle 11 615 Feb 28, 2023 JavaScript TypeScript Array Runtime - Beats \n76 ms - 92.39% \n Memory - Beats \n45.5 MB - 97.76% \n Here we use an Array of a fixed length and insert the number of the nums array to the ans array. \nBy inserting the number in both destinations at once we're more efficient than e.g. spreading the array twice. \n The creation of the fixed length array also reduces a lot internal copying compared to a \".push()\" solution. \n Code \n function   getConcatenation ( nums :   number [ ] ) :   number [ ]   { \n      const  len  =  nums . length ; \n      const  ans  =   new   Array ( 2   *  len ) ; \n   \n      for   ( let  i  =   0 ;  i  <  len ;  i ++ )   { \n         ans [ i ]   =  nums [ i ] ; \n         ans [ i  +  len ]   =  nums [ i ] ; \n      } \n   \n      return  ans ; \n } ; Comments (1) cattleherd Jan 03, 2024 big brain my dude. You allocate the memory ahead of time, and its more predictable then relying on javascript push algorithm. 0"
